The scapula's muscles and the moving base of the arm (@shoulder-scapular-muscles: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @shoulder, #source @med-source-osu, #source @med-source-shoulder-muscles, #medicine @shoulder-neurovascular, #orthopedics @shoulder-neurovascular, #shoulder @shoulder-neurovascular, #source @med-source-applied) { r_636ZTXMHVB85FX3DGRYHA3QYAB
**Revisit the relevant part of the regional section:** [Anatomy & Physiology — Oregon State University](../../../sources/Anatomy-and-Physiology.lingua#r_1WEZWZ5YY6797K4EXVYTBJS25N), the muscles that position the pectoral girdle within chapter 11. Draw their attachments and compare the action table with [Shoulder muscles — NCBI Bookshelf](../../../sources/Shoulder-muscles.lingua#r_76PY1TN1ZHB847YGG80ZCB5S50). In [Applied Human Anatomy — Nolan and McNamara](../../../sources/Applied-Human-Anatomy.lingua#r_2JW0W235R6C2JBECH8N4WP5N3K), read chapter 2 Movement items 1–2 on printed page 31 as muscle-and-nerve questions.

Trapezius/trapézio and serratus anterior/serrátil anterior help coordinate scapular positioning and upward rotation. Different trapezius portions have different contributions; coordinated action cannot be reduced to one muscle lifting the scapula.

Rhomboids/romboides and levator scapulae/levantador da escápula connect the scapula with the vertebral region. Pectoralis minor/peitoral menor connects ribs and coracoid, influencing scapular position. Subclavius/subclávio lies beneath the clavicle. The chest wall is a moving reference rather than a rigid drawing board.

Scapular movements include elevation/elevação, depression/depressão, protraction/protração, retraction/retração, and upward and downward rotation/rotação superior e inferior. Three-dimensional descriptions also include tilt and internal or external rotation.

**Try:** draw where each named muscle attaches to the scapula or clavicle. Predict a direction of pull, then check the action in the source. Explain why visible asymmetry alone does not prove that one named muscle is weak or that it causes a person's pain.

